  "strategy": "# 1. Approach and Rationale\nThe goal is to find a step function h that minimizes the overlap integral for the Erd\u0151s problem. The previous solution used gradient-based optimization, which may be trapped in local minima due to the non-convexity of the problem. The new approach employs Simulated Annealing (SA), a stochastic optimization method effective for escaping local optima in non-convex problems. SA iteratively explores the solution space by accepting worse solutions probabilistically, controlled by a cooling schedule.\n\n# 2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, n = number of points\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k (h * (1 - h_k)) where h_k is h shifted by k\n- Constraints: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u21d2 sum(h) * dx = 1\n- Cooling schedule: Temperature T starts high and decreases over iterations\n\n# 3. Implementation Procedure\n## Initialization\n1. Get initial_h_values if available\n2. Else, initialize h uniformly: h[i] = 0.5 for all i\n3. Ensure feasibility: h = project_to_feasible(h)\n\n## SA Loop\n1. Set initial temperature T = T_initial\n2. For each iteration until budget exhausted:\n   a. Compute current cost (C\u2085)\n   b. Generate neighbor h' by perturbing h\n   c. Compute cost \u0394 = new_cost - current_cost\n   d. Accept h' with probability exp(-\u0394/T)\n   e. Update h and T (cooling)\n\n## Perturbation\n1. Randomly select a small subset of points to perturb\n2. For each point, set h[i] to random value in [0,1]\n3. Adjust remaining points to maintain integral constraint\n\n## Cooling Schedule\n1. Use exponential decay: T = T * \u03b1\n2. \u03b1 = 0.99, starting T = 1000\n\n## Feasibility Projection\n1. Clip h to [0,1]\n2. Rescale to ensure sum(h) = n_points / 2\n\n## Termination\n1. Stop when time budget exhausted\n2. Return best h found\n\n# 4. Search Choices and Variations\n## Perturbation Strategies\n- Random point mutation (default)\n- Block swapping (alternate variation)\n## Cooling Rates\n- Fast cooling (\u03b1=0.95) for exploration\n- Slow cooling (\u03b1=0.99) for exploitation\n\n# 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n## Time Estimates\n- Each iteration: ~0.1s\n- 10,000 iterations: ~1000s\n- Include feasibility checks and cost evaluations\n\n## Robustness\n- Handle infeasible solutions by projecting\n- Track best solution continuously\n- Adapt cooling rate based on progress\n\n# 6. Validation and Return Contract\n## Checks\n1. 0 <= h[i] <= 1\n2. sum(h) = n_points / 2\n3. C\u2085 computed correctly\n4. Return within budget\n\n## Final Return\nReturn (h, C\u2085, n_points) ensuring h is feasible and C\u2085 is minimal",
